==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=Multiplier}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|mŭlʹ-tĭ-plī'-ər}}, {{IPA|/ˈmʌltɪˌplaɪər/|lang=en}}
** {{a|UK}} {{IPA|[ˈmʌɫ.tɪ.ˌplaɪ.ə(ɹ)]|lang=en}}
** {{a|US}} {{IPA|[ˈmʌɫ.tɪ.ˌplaɪ.ɚ]|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|mul|ti|pli|er|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|arithmetic}} A [[number]] by which another (the [[multiplicand]]) is to be multiplied.
#: ''In the expression'' 5 × 7, ''the "5" is a '''multiplier'''.''
#: ''The score '''multiplier''' increases each time you complete a level of the video game.''
# {{label|en|grammar}} An [[adjective]] indicating the number of times something is to be multiplied.
#: ''English '''multipliers''' include "double" and "triple".''
# {{label|en|economics}} A [[ratio]] used to estimate total economic effect for a variety of economic activities.
# {{label|en|physics}} Any of several [[device]]s used to enhance a [[signal]]
## {{label|en|physics|obsolete}} a [[coil]]; when [[w:Johann Schweigger|Johann Schweigger]] in 1820 invented the electric coil, increasing the electro-magnetic field from a single wire, this invention was called a ''multiplier''.
# A [[multiplier onion]].
#* '''1995''', Marian Coonse, ''Onions, Leeks and Garlic: A Handbook for Gardeners'' (page 52)
#*: Shallots are the most prolific of all the '''multipliers''', and easier to grow than onions.

